{"name":"Miyamoto Red","hex":"#e4030f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shigeru Miyamoto","blocks":[{"text":"Shigeru Miyamoto  is a Japanese video game designer, producer, game director and filmmaker at Nintendo, where he has served as one of its representative directors as an executive since 2002. Widely regarded as one of the most accomplished and influential designers in video games, he is the creator of some of the most acclaimed and best-selling game franchises of all time, including Mario, The Legend of Zelda, Donkey Kong, Star Fox and Pikmin. More than 1 billion copies of games featuring franchises created by Miyamoto have been sold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Shigeru_Miyamoto_20150610_%28cropped_4%29.jpg/330px-Shigeru_Miyamoto_20150610_%28cropped_4%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shigeru_Miyamoto","wikidata":"Q12382"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Kanazawa_College_of_Art.jpg/500px-Kanazawa_College_of_Art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Kanazawa_College_of_Art.jpg/1280px-Kanazawa_College_of_Art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Kanazawa_College_of_Art.jpg/500px-Kanazawa_College_of_Art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Miyamoto graduated from Kanazawa College of Art in Ishikawa Prefecture.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKanazawa_College_of_Art.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/NES-Console-Set.jpg/500px-NES-Console-Set.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/NES-Console-Set.jpg/1280px-NES-Console-Set.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/NES-Console-Set.jpg/500px-NES-Console-Set.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Miyamoto's Super Mario Bros. was bundled with the NES in America. The game and the system are credited with helping to bring North America out of the slump of the 1983 game industry crash.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANES-Console-Set.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/SNES-Controller-in-Hand.jpg/500px-SNES-Controller-in-Hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/SNES-Controller-in-Hand.jpg/1280px-SNES-Controller-in-Hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/SNES-Controller-in-Hand.jpg/500px-SNES-Controller-in-Hand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Miyamoto was responsible for the controller design of the Super Famicom/Nintendo. Its L/R buttons were an industry first and have since become commonplace.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASNES-Controller-in-Hand.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Miyamoto-san_conductor_E3.jpg/500px-Miyamoto-san_conductor_E3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Miyamoto-san_conductor_E3.jpg/500px-Miyamoto-san_conductor_E3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Miyamoto holding up a Wii Remote at E3 2006","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMiyamoto-san_conductor_E3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Shigeru_Miyamoto_at_E3_2013_3_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Shigeru_Miyamoto_at_E3_2013_3_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Shigeru_Miyamoto_at_E3_2013_3_%28cropped%29.JPG/1280px-Shigeru_Miyamoto_at_E3_2013_3_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Shigeru_Miyamoto_at_E3_2013_3_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Shigeru_Miyamoto_at_E3_2013_3_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","al